\title{Both leaders keep caches across declarations and check each declaration soon after its dependencies}
\taxon{Insight}
\date{2026-11-23}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{arena}
\tag{driver}
\tag{machine}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/04-leader-nano-lean.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Source: [study-nano-lean](study-nano-lean) and [study-gruszka-pestka](study-gruszka-pestka); [the research session](ses-XOS5). Read from the sources and the notes of the two projects; their figures are their own.}
\ul{
\li{nano-lean keeps its caches inside a session per worker, dropped only when the session arena passes 64 MiB or a declaration fails, and reorders declarations at load time so that each one follows soon after its dependencies. Closed sub-terms of the export are evaluated and checked once per session, not once per use.}
\li{gruszka frees memory by truncating arrays at the end of a session of 8 MiB. Its sessions only move forward in file order, free variables are named by their type so that binders opened again hit the caches, and each worker takes a contiguous part of the file.}
\li{The measured worth, by their authors: -9 percent for namespace order, -18 to -20 percent and a further -4 to -5 percent for the naming of variables on Mathlib slices; -5 percent for contiguous parts against round robin.}
\li{Two thirds of the expressions of Mathlib are reachable from exactly one proof. gruszka converts the export once to a binary file and decodes those expressions into a worker-local arena only when the theorem is checked.}
\li{nano-lean replaces the unbounded argument comparison of lazy delta by budgeted same-head probes with a failure cache, and gives a definition that was unfolded 32 times a derived reduction rule.}
}
\p{RLVM has the same three things: sessions, free variables that are canonical by type and depth, and proof blocks that stay packed until their theorem is checked. The canonical variable is also where a table keyed by values first departed from the official caches, see [the cache order](ins-PO1G), and the right size of a session turned out to be [a property of the cache](ins-FJ5R).}
\p{See also: [warm sessions and the session cap](design-warm-sessions).}
